import ast
out=Path('output/pdf/StegoVerify_FR7_Code_Trace_and_Demo_Guide.pdf')
def ref(file,name):
 tree=ast.parse(Path(file).read_text(encoding='utf-8-sig'))
 for node in ast.walk(tree):
  if isinstance(node,(ast.FunctionDef,ast.ClassDef)) and node.name==name:
   return f'{file}:{node.lineno} ({name})'
 return file+' ('+name+')'
def sources(*items):
 p('<b>Code to open:</b> '+'; '.join(ref(*x) for x in items),'CellX')
def page(title,subtitle):
 if story: story.append(PageBreak())
 p(title,'TitleX');p(subtitle,'Deck')
def qa(q,a): p('<b>'+q+'</b><br/>'+a)
page('Variable start location in your code','Zharfan\'s detailed FR7 / FR13 demo guide | Checked against the supplied code')
p('<b>How it works:</b> the program embeds a hidden container beginning at a selectable unit in a PNG image or PCM WAV. Manual mode converts your coordinates into a unit index. Keyed mode calculates an index from a shared secret, masked cover fingerprint, media type and LSB depth. The receiver repeats the calculation and checks what is at that position.')
p('This guide traces actual functions, explains each input and includes measured examples. Code references use the current project files and line numbers; these can change after edits. The attached explanation was treated as reference material, not as instructions about who should present.')
h('Is the attached PDF correct? Mostly, with these corrections')
table([['<b>Claim in the attached PDF</b>','<b>Accurate explanation</b>'],['The payload does not start at the first pixel.','It is not fixed there. Keyed modulo can return unit 0; manual mode can also select the first pixel.'],['Receiver needs the received file and nothing else.','Keyed derivation needs the shared secret and an LSB depth selected or tried automatically. Full verification also needs the matching trusted public key.'],['The position is never stored anywhere.','The numeric start is not an explicit field in the embedded record. It is stored temporarily in result objects and GUI state, and can appear in output/logs. Manual coordinates must be shared separately.'],['The payload follows 16 bytes after the header starts.','The record begins at byte 16. The user message begins after the record and signature too: offset 16 + record length + signature length.'],['The masked hash behaves exactly like a salt.','It is deterministic, content-derived input to HMAC, not a random salt. The random encryption salt belongs to a separate scrypt operation.'],['Changing the secret, file or depth always moves the start.','It usually changes it, but different inputs can have the same modulo result. Changes only in masked bits leave that fingerprint unchanged.'],['True / False directly gives the verdict.','It records found-at-expected status. Signature and integrity checks run before a location mismatch becomes Wrong Start Location.']],[207,296])
p('<b>Keep:</b> the HMAC formula, masked-hash idea, whole-container layout and diagnostic scanning explanation. The measured image starts 358,456 and 141,331 in the attachment were reproduced with the current sample and its two stated secrets.')
p('<b>Source reviewed:</b> StegoVerify_VariableStartLocation_Explained.pdf, pages 1-4. The wording about the secret protecting "where" should be qualified: the visible marker still allows scanning.')

page('From clicking Keyed to calling the function','The GUI collects choices; engine.protect coordinates the actual work')
code('app.py: main() -> App() -> app.mainloop()\n\nKeyed radio button -> p_mode = "keyed"\n                    -> p_mode_changed() updates visible controls\n\nPROTECT button -> App.p_run()\n               -> engine.protect(..., mode, secret, n_lsb, ...)\n               -> payload.build_record(...)\n               -> startloc.derive_start(...)\n               -> crypto_utils.hmac_sha256(...)\n               -> lsb.embed(...) -> save output')
h('1  Selecting Keyed does not yet embed anything')
p('In <b>app.py, lines 293-306</b>, p_mode is a Tkinter StringVar, initially MODE_KEYED. The radio button writes its value to that variable. p_mode_changed() shows the secret entry or manual coordinate controls. p_secret holds the text you enter; show="*" only masks its appearance on screen.')
h('2  Protect reads the GUI values')
code('n = int(self.p_n.get())\nmode = self.p_mode.get()\nmanual = self.p_manual_unit(cover) if mode == ... else None\nr = engine.protect(..., n, self.priv_key, mode=mode,\n                   secret=self.p_secret.get(),\n                   manual_unit=manual, passphrase=passphrase, ...)')
p('This is an abbreviated call from <b>App.p_run()</b>. self means the current App object; .get() reads a Tkinter field. The keyword argument secret= passes the text to the engine. manual is None in keyed mode. The signing private key and optional encryption passphrase are separate inputs.')
h('3  The engine prepares the record before deriving the start')
p('<b>engine.protect()</b> validates n, loads the cover, checks capacity and copies the cover. <b>payload.build_record()</b> calls cover.masked_hash(n_lsb), stores its hexadecimal form in record["hash"], and creates message hashes and metadata. The engine signs and packs the container, then enters its keyed/manual branch.')
code('if mode == startloc.MODE_KEYED:\n    start = startloc.derive_start(\n        secret, cover.kind, bytes.fromhex(record["hash"]),\n        n_lsb, cover.num_units)')
p('<b>Where the arguments come from:</b> secret is the GUI text; cover.kind is image/audio from the adapter; record["hash"] was computed from the original cover; n_lsb is your selector; num_units is the number of usable channel/sample bytes. bytes.fromhex converts the 64-character hash string back to 32 binary bytes.')
sources(('app.py','p_mode_changed'),('app.py','p_run'),('stegoverify/engine.py','protect'),('stegoverify/payload.py','build_record'))

page('How the masked fingerprint is made','covers.py supplies the stable value used by both sender and receiver')
h('The mask removes exactly the selected low bits')
code('def _mask_table(n_lsb):\n    keep = 0xFF ^ ((1 << n_lsb) - 1)\n    return bytes(b & keep for b in range(256))')
p('<b>For n = 2:</b> 1 &lt;&lt; 2 gives 4; subtracting 1 gives binary 00000011. XOR with 0xFF (11111111) gives <b>11111100</b>. AND with that mask preserves the upper six bits and clears the last two. The function precomputes this result for every possible byte value, 0-255.')
code('Original:       10110101 -> masked: 10110100\nAfter embedding:10110110 -> masked: 10110100\n\nDifferent embedded bits, identical input to the hash.')
h('ImageCover.masked_hash, explained')
code('h = hashlib.sha256()\nh.update(b"SVFY-IMG")\nh.update(struct.pack(">IIB", width, height, channels))\nh.update(bytes(self.units()).translate(_mask_table(n_lsb)))\n# If present, alpha bytes are also hashed unchanged.\nreturn h.digest()')
p('The snippet above shortens self.width/self.height/self.img.channels for readability. <b>hashlib.sha256()</b> creates the hash object. Each <b>update()</b> feeds more bytes into the same calculation. The label identifies image data; struct.pack encodes dimensions and channel count into bytes. <b>translate()</b> applies the lookup table to every unit. <b>digest()</b> returns the 32-byte SHA-256 fingerprint.')
p('Image units exclude alpha, so transparency is not overwritten. Alpha is still included unchanged in the masked hash. The hash represents decoded image content and selected structure, not every byte of the compressed PNG file.')
h('AudioCover.masked_hash uses the same principle')
p('The WAV implementation includes sample width, channel count and sample rate. It makes a temporary copy of the audio bytes and masks only the lowest byte of each channel sample. Higher bytes are included unchanged. At 16-bit PCM, embedding never overwrites the upper sample byte.')
p('<b>Why verification agrees:</b> normal embedding changes only the lowest n bits of units. Masking those bits on the original and saved stego file produces the same hash input. The file on disk is not cleared. Sender and receiver therefore get the same fingerprint if the other represented content is intact.')
p('<b>Limit:</b> masked bits are excluded everywhere, including outside the container. At 8 LSB in an RGB image, colour values contribute no distinguishing bits to this media hash. Signed message hashes protect the payload separately.')
sources(('stegoverify/covers.py','_mask_table'),('stegoverify/covers.py','masked_hash'))
p('Audio counterpart: covers.py:183, AudioCover.masked_hash.','CellX')

page('The HMAC function line by line','Project code assembles the inputs; Python supplies the HMAC implementation')
code('def derive_start(secret, cover_kind, masked_hash,\n                 n_lsb, num_units):\n    if not secret:\n        raise ValueError("a start-location secret is required")\n    msg = (b"SVFY-start|" + cover_kind.encode() + b"|"\n           + masked_hash + bytes([n_lsb]))\n    mac = cu.hmac_sha256(secret.encode("utf-8"), msg)\n    return int.from_bytes(mac, "big") % num_units')
p('This is startloc.py:42 with type hints omitted, the long expression wrapped, and the error text shortened. The operations are unchanged.')
qa('What does if not secret do?','Rejects an empty or missing secret. It does not enforce a strong password. Whitespace is not removed by this function. The code has no explicit maximum secret length.')
qa('What does b"SVFY-start|" mean?','A fixed byte-string label for this use of HMAC. It is public, not a secret or random salt. The vertical bars inside the strings are literal separator bytes; + joins the bytes together.')
qa('What does cover_kind.encode() do?','Converts image or audio from text to UTF-8 bytes. The media label is not converted into units here; the cover adapter already provides units separately.')
qa('What does bytes([n_lsb]) do?','Encodes n as one numeric byte. At n = 1 it is byte 0x01, not the text character "1". The masked_hash argument is already 32 binary bytes.')
qa('What does secret.encode("utf-8") do?','Converts the shared secret text into key bytes. This is encoding, not encryption. It is passed as the HMAC key; msg is passed as the data.')
h('Where the HMAC code comes from')
code('# startloc.py imports the project helper as cu\nfrom . import crypto_utils as cu\n\n# crypto_utils.py imports Python standard-library modules\nimport hmac\nimport hashlib\n\ndef hmac_sha256(key, msg):\n    return hmac.new(key, msg, hashlib.sha256).digest()')
p('The dot in the import means the same stegoverify package. <b>cu</b> is an alias. The project helper selects SHA-256 and calls Python\'s HMAC implementation; the team has not written the HMAC cryptographic algorithm from scratch. HMAC is a keyed hash construction, not just SHA256(secret + data), and not encryption. digest returns 32 bytes.')
p('<b>int.from_bytes(mac, "big")</b> interprets those bytes as one unsigned number, with the first byte most significant. <b>% num_units</b> takes its remainder, giving an index from 0 to N - 1. For illustration only: 1,234 % 100 = 34. No decryption is involved.')
sources(('stegoverify/startloc.py','derive_start'),('stegoverify/crypto_utils.py','hmac_sha256'))

page('Where the data is actually written','Units, manual coordinates, header layout and wrap-around')
h('A start unit is not a file-byte offset')
p('<b>Image:</b> a unit is a decoded colour-channel byte. A 512 x 384 RGB image has 589,824 units. A keyed start may fall on R, G or B. Manual pixel selection starts at that pixel\'s first colour channel.')
code('Image manual unit = (y * width + x) * colour_channels\nPixel (300, 300) in 512-wide RGB -> unit 461,700\n\nAudio manual unit = frame_index * channels\nStereo frame 20,000 -> unit 40,000')
p('<b>Audio:</b> each unit is a channel sample\'s lowest byte. The GUI field called sample index counts time frames across all channels; a stereo frame contains two units. Both converters validate bounds. The engine manual branch uses manual_unit directly and does not call derive_start.')
h('The whole container begins at that unit')
code('Hidden container byte layout:\n[16-byte header][JSON record][64-byte signature][message][CRC]\n\nHeader: SVFY | version | flags | three section lengths\nUser-message byte offset = 16 + record_length + signature_length')
p('The LSB count is in the signed JSON record, not the 16-byte header. The numeric start and secret are not explicit record fields. All container bits are embedded in order from the chosen unit; the header is not stored separately at a fixed location. This offset is measured in container bytes, not image pixels.')
h('The engine passes the position to the LSB module')
code('units = stego.units()\nchanged = lsb.embed(units, container, start, n_lsb)\nstego.set_units(units)\nstego.save(out_path)')
p('<b>bytes_to_chunks()</b> splits container bits into chunks of n bits. <b>embed()</b> clears the lowest n bits of each selected unit and ORs in its next chunk. A payload bit already equal to the cover bit does not visibly change it, so the first numerically changed unit need not be exactly the start.')
p('<b>_wrapped_indices()</b> handles a start near the end: write to the end, then continue at unit zero. Example: N = 10, start = 8, four chunks use units 8, 9, 0, 1. Capacity checks reject containers needing more than N units, preventing overlap.')
p('<b>Why the start does not reduce capacity:</b> usable bytes = floor(N x n / 8), regardless of start. The position only chooses where this cyclic sequence begins. It does not randomly scatter all the bits.')
sources(('app.py','p_manual_unit'),('stegoverify/covers.py','pixel_to_unit'),('stegoverify/covers.py','sample_to_unit'),('stegoverify/lsb.py','embed'),('stegoverify/lsb.py','_wrapped_indices'))

page('How Verify finds and checks the location','The expected start is calculated before reading the hidden record')
code('VERIFY -> App.v_run() -> engine.verify()\n  -> load received cover and get units\n  -> choose n, or try n = 1..8 in auto mode\n  -> calculate expected start\n  -> _locate(): header here? otherwise optional scan\n  -> extract container and check integrity/signature\n  -> compare actual position with expected position\n  -> open message and check its plaintext hash')
h('The receiver calls the same derive_start function')
code('expected = startloc.derive_start(\n    secret, cover.kind, cover.masked_hash(n),\n    n, cover.num_units)')
p('Here cover is the received stego object. Its type and unit count come from loading the file, and its masked hash is recomputed directly. The decoder does not need to extract record["hash"] first. This avoids a circular dependency. The user supplies n or auto tries it; the signed lsb_bits field is available only after extraction.')
h('_locate checks a candidate header')
code('head = lsb.extract(units, expected, n_lsb, pl.HEADER_LEN)\nreturn (expected, *pl.parse_header(head), True)')
p('<b>extract()</b> collects enough n-bit chunks to reconstruct 16 bytes; at 1 LSB that requires 128 units. <b>parse_header()</b> checks SVFY, version and plausible lengths. The * expands returned fields into the tuple. True means found at expected, not Authentic.')
p('If that fails and scan is enabled, <b>find_pattern()</b> converts SVFY to n-bit chunks and searches the cover\'s low-bit stream, including wrap-around. It returns up to eight candidate hits by default. _locate tries their headers and returns the first that parses, marked False. This is a diagnostic search, not proof that every possible container was examined.')
h('Why the final verdict is not immediate')
p('After locating, verify checks container size, CRC/JSON, Ed25519 signature, stored-message hash and masked media hash. A failure may yield Tampered or Signature Invalid before the location check. Only a genuine, intact container found at the wrong position produces <b>Wrong Start Location</b>.')
code('if not at_expected:\n    res.verdict, res.reason = WRONG_START, ...\n    return res\n# Only after this does open_message(...) run.')
p('No header found produces Payload Missing. A wrong secret with scan disabled can also produce Payload Missing. With an encrypted payload, the passphrase is separate: a valid container can be Authentic but unreadable if decryption fails. For a successful demo, show recovered content and payload hash MATCH.')
sources(('app.py','v_run'),('stegoverify/engine.py','verify'),('stegoverify/engine.py','_locate'),('stegoverify/lsb.py','find_pattern'),('stegoverify/payload.py','parse_header'))

